## Control {{controlId}}: Apply Deny-by-Exception (Blocklisting) or Deny-All, Permit-by-Exception (Allowlisting) to Prevent Unauthorized Software Execution

**Implementation Status:** {{status}}

This organization applies application control policies within the {{tenantName}} M365 GCC High tenant through Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC), Intune application protection policies, and software installation restrictions managed by {{variables.systemAdministrator}}.

{{#if appProtectionsActive}}
**Application Protection Policies:**
{{appProtectionsCount}} application protection policies enforce software execution controls:
- Managed applications restricted to approved list (permit-by-exception)
- Unmanaged application data transfer blocked via DLP controls
- Jailbreak/root detection prevents execution on compromised devices
{{/if}}

{{#if detectedAppsMonitored}}
**Software Authorization Monitoring:**
{{detectedAppsCount}} applications detected across the managed fleet are continuously monitored:
{{#if apps}}
{{#each apps}}
- **{{displayName}}** v{{version}} — Publisher: {{publisher}} — Installed on {{deviceCount}} devices
{{/each}}
{{/if}}

Applications not on the authorized software list are flagged for investigation and removal per the unauthorized software remediation procedure.
{{else}}
Application detection is not active. Software authorization monitoring is not verified.
{{/if}}

{{#if deviceConfigurationsActive}}
**Endpoint Application Control:**
WDAC policies deployed via {{deviceConfigurationsCount}} device configurations enforce:
- Code integrity policies restricting execution to signed, authorized applications
- Script enforcement preventing unauthorized PowerShell, VBScript, and batch file execution
- Managed installer rules allowing only Intune-deployed applications
{{/if}}

**Responsible Party:** {{variables.systemAdministrator}}

**Verification Method:** Examine WDAC policy configurations, application protection policies, detected application reports, and unauthorized software remediation logs.

